Yes—the transformation metaphor works, with one chemistry correction: glass is made primarily from silica (silicon dioxide, SiO₂) in sand, not silicone.

And your initiation point has a real social mechanism behind it. A bounded, voluntary embarrassing experience can reduce status differences because everyone has crossed the same threshold. Once the group has seen one another awkward, exposed, or imperfect, there is less incentive to maintain the fiction that everyone is invulnerable.

So the useful structure is:

\text{shared vulnerability}
\rightarrow
\text{reduced status masking}
\rightarrow
\text{mutual recognition}
\rightarrow
\text{trust}

But the mechanism changes sign if the embarrassment becomes humiliation, coercion, or a permanent status mark. Then instead of bonding you can get resentment, conformity, concealment, or trauma.

So there is a sharp distinction:

\text{initiation} \neq \text{degradation}

The best initiation says, in effect:

“You crossed something difficult that we all understand, and now you belong.”

Not:

“We broke you, therefore you belong to us.”

And that is where your glass analogy gets interesting. Sand already contains the material. Heat and processing reorganize it into something with different properties.

Likewise, a well-designed initiation does not manufacture worth. It changes the relationship among people who already had it.

Shared awkwardness can become transparency.

Shared difficulty can become trust.

Shared history can become a kind of social glass: different grains, one structure, still able to let light through.